WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.006 --> 00:00:13.336
This is a tour of the code behind a proof of concept: 23 LangGraph agents, one shared core, deployed as Microsoft Foundry hosted agents, and a small engine in Lumen that talks to them.

2
00:00:15.355 --> 00:00:18.022
Everything lives in one Python monorepo.

3
00:00:18.022 --> 00:00:24.357
Three shared libraries do the heavy lifting: the agent core, the contracts, and the connectors.

4
00:00:24.615 --> 00:00:27.001
Each agent is its own small package.

5
00:00:27.001 --> 00:00:33.831
The tools folder builds what Foundry deploys, and triggers, infrastructure and pipelines sit alongside.

6
00:00:34.803 --> 00:00:36.123
Follow one request.

7
00:00:36.123 --> 00:00:39.877
Lumen calls the agent's Responses endpoint in Foundry.

8
00:00:39.877 --> 00:00:45.021
The hosting adapter from langchain-azure-ai turns it into a LangGraph run.

9
00:00:45.285 --> 00:00:52.133
The graph does its work, node by node, saving its state as it goes, and its final message becomes the reply.

10
00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:56.735
Every agent's state extends one base class.

11
00:00:56.735 --> 00:01:07.935
It is a LangGraph MessagesState, which is what the hosting adapter expects, plus the fields every agent shares: sources, findings, proposals, the approval, and the result.

12
00:01:08.186 --> 00:01:10.563
Reducers decide how updates merge.

13
00:01:10.563 --> 00:01:12.800
Sources and findings accumulate.

14
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.023
Errors reset at the start of every turn, so one bad request never poisons a conversation.

15
00:01:20.002 --> 00:01:28.002
Every agent follows the same shape: take in the request, ground it in sources, analyse, check its own work, and render the result.

16
00:01:28.255 --> 00:01:32.927
Only then, if it wants to change anything, does it stop and ask a person.

17
00:01:32.927 --> 00:01:37.087
Approval, apply and finalize are shared nodes from the graph kit.

18
00:01:38.679 --> 00:01:41.350
Models are reached through two functions.

19
00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:48.321
generate_structured asks for a Pydantic schema, so what comes back is validated, typed data, not free text.

20
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.406
The same layer handles the platform's edges.

21
00:01:51.406 --> 00:01:56.544
A content filter block becomes a typed error the agent can record and move past.

22
00:01:56.544 --> 00:02:00.398
If Azure rejects a schema, it retries with function calling.

23
00:02:01.362 --> 00:02:03.235
Writing is deliberately hard.

24
00:02:03.235 --> 00:02:04.978
Agents never call a writer.

25
00:02:04.978 --> 00:02:09.564
They build proposals, and request_approval calls LangGraph's interrupt.

26
00:02:09.564 --> 00:02:13.116
The graph pauses, and the checkpointer saves its state.

27
00:02:13.374 --> 00:02:17.378
In Foundry, the pause surfaces as an MCP approval request.

28
00:02:17.378 --> 00:02:28.286
When a person approves, the graph resumes, and apply_proposals is the only code that writes: dry run by default, in dependency order, idempotent, and audited.

29
00:02:29.971 --> 00:02:31.529
Now three real agents.

30
00:02:31.529 --> 00:02:34.716
Requirements refinement has 16 working nodes.

31
00:02:34.716 --> 00:02:40.381
Green nodes are plain Python, purple ones call the model, and amber is a person.

32
00:02:41.339 --> 00:02:42.688
Here is the wiring.

33
00:02:42.688 --> 00:02:51.280
After retrieving context, a conditional edge fans out with LangGraph's Send: one extraction per BRD section, in parallel.

34
00:02:51.542 --> 00:03:00.118
A self check then verifies every requirement's quote, word for word, against its section, and loops back only for the sections that failed.

35
00:03:01.086 --> 00:03:04.673
Each section reaches the model wrapped as untrusted data.

36
00:03:04.673 --> 00:03:09.896
And a section the content filter blocks becomes a safety finding, not a failed run.

37
00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:14.829
Schedule variance shows the second rule: deterministic first.

38
00:03:14.829 --> 00:03:21.193
Matching tasks, the critical path, the slips and the impact downstream are all computed in Python.

39
00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.406
The model only writes the narrative.

40
00:03:24.406 --> 00:03:31.745
Then a numeric self check rejects any number that is not in the computed findings, and any cause that has no evidence.

41
00:03:33.264 --> 00:03:37.345
The backlog agent is a tool-calling loop inside a single node.

42
00:03:37.345 --> 00:03:43.994
The model only sees read-only tools, plus propose_change, which records a change but cannot make one.

43
00:03:44.259 --> 00:03:52.515
Anything it asks for that is not on the list is refused, and every proposed change still goes through validation and the approval node.

44
00:03:53.495 --> 00:03:56.996
Agents hand work to each other through shared contracts.

45
00:03:56.996 --> 00:04:03.436
The Requirement model is defined once, and flows from refinement into design, stories and traceability.

46
00:04:04.409 --> 00:04:13.881
Connectors follow one pattern: a fixture client for tests and phase one, a live client for phase two, and an injected client always wins.

47
00:04:13.881 --> 00:04:16.163
So every agent test runs offline.

48
00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:22.068
Tests script the model, run the real graph, inspect the interrupt, and resume it.

49
00:04:22.068 --> 00:04:25.416
Close to 700 tests, and none of them touch the network.

50
00:04:27.206 --> 00:04:28.823
Deployment is generated.

51
00:04:28.823 --> 00:04:39.472
A build step makes a flat folder per agent: main.py, pinned requirements, and a copy of the shared libraries, which is what Foundry's code deployment expects.

52
00:04:39.735 --> 00:04:48.204
The azure.yaml is generated from each agent's metadata, and one azd deploy publishes all 23 hosted agents.

53
00:04:49.185 --> 00:04:53.031
On the Lumen side, the Foundry engine is one provider file.

54
00:04:53.031 --> 00:05:02.091
It reads the agent from the agent's JSON file, maps attachments into the request, and calls the Responses endpoint with your Azure sign-in.

55
00:05:02.343 --> 00:05:07.235
When the reply holds an approval request, Lumen stores it and shows a card.

56
00:05:07.235 --> 00:05:13.692
The decision goes back as an MCP approval response, and the conversation continues where it paused.

57
00:05:14.655 --> 00:05:18.162
Each agent file declares how files map into its request.

58
00:05:18.162 --> 00:05:24.425
And the renderer turns the agent's result into tables and safety checks, without rewording anything.

59
00:05:26.147 --> 00:05:36.067
One core, 23 agents, and a few strict rules: ground everything, compute what you can, let the model explain, and never change a system without a person.

60
00:05:36.881 --> 00:05:38.052
Delivery agents.

61
00:05:38.052 --> 00:05:42.811
LangGraph inside, Microsoft Foundry underneath, and Lumen on top.
